US Hospital Readmission Project — Cleaning & Statistical Decisions Log
Documented from the beginning of the project up to 3 November 2026.
1. Data sources and raw tables
- HRRP dataset imported into public.hrrp_raw with all original columns initially stored as TEXT.
- Hospital General Information dataset imported into public.hospital_general_information_raw with all original columns initially stored as TEXT.
- Raw tables are preserved separately from cleaned and analysis tables.
- HRRP raw dataset contains 18,330 records across 3,055 facilities and six readmission measures.
- Hospital General Information raw dataset contains 5,419 records.
- Facility ID selected as the common identifier for joining HRRP and hospital information datasets.
2. HRRP cleaning decisions
- Created public.hrrp_clean as the cleaned HRRP dataset.
- Renamed original columns into lowercase snake_case for easier SQL querying and automation.
- Converted facility_id to BIGINT.
- Converted number_of_discharges to INTEGER.
- Converted number_of_readmissions to INTEGER where numeric values were available.
- Converted Excess Readmission Ratio to NUMERIC(12,6).
- Converted Predicted Readmission Rate to NUMERIC(12,6).
- Converted Expected Readmission Rate to NUMERIC(12,6).
- Converted Start Date and End Date into DATE columns in the clean-table schema.
- Preserved footnote information in the HRRP clean table.
- Checked duplicate records using Facility ID and Measure Name; no duplicate hospital-measure combinations were found.
- Retained all six HRRP measures rather than restricting the dataset to a single condition.
- Identified three original readmission-count categories: numeric values, N/A, and Too Few to Report.
- Converted numeric readmission counts into INTEGER values while retaining unavailable or suppressed counts as NULL in the numeric column.
- Decided to preserve the original status separately using a TEXT display column.
- Standardized Too Few to Report to <10 for dashboard display.
- Preserved N/A as N/A rather than treating it as zero.
- Kept unmatched HRRP records as NULL rather than converting them to N/A.
- Identified 6,610 N/A records and 3,683 Too Few to Report records in the original HRRP dataset.
- Identified 8,037 records with numeric readmission counts.
3. Hospital General Information cleaning decisions
- Created public.hospital_general_information_clean from the raw hospital information table.
- Standardized column names into lowercase snake_case.
- Converted Facility ID into BIGINT.
- Converted Hospital Overall Rating into INTEGER.
- Converted applicable count fields into INTEGER.
- Standardized missing and unavailable values during cleaning.
- Removed records without a valid Facility ID.
- Removed duplicate hospital records.
- Retained 5,255 records in the cleaned hospital information table.
- Created public.hospital_information_analysis as a reduced hospital information table.
- Retained Facility ID, Facility Name, Address, City, State, County, Hospital Type, Hospital Ownership, Emergency Services, Birthing-Friendly Designation, and Hospital Overall Rating.
- Removed Telephone Number from the analysis table.
- Removed ZIP Code from the analysis table.
- Removed all footnote columns from the analysis table.
- Excluded MORT, Safety, READM, Patient Experience (Pt Exp), and Timely and Effective Care (TE) measure groups from this analysis table.
- Decided not to use the General Information READM summary group as a substitute for the six HRRP measures because the measures are not identical.
4. Statistical and readmission-rate decisions
- Decided not to calculate national or state readmission rates using simple averages of hospital-level percentages.
- Weighted aggregation using appropriate discharge denominators was selected as the preferred approach wherever the required data is available.
- Defined the calculated crude readmission rate as:
Number of Readmissions / Number of Discharges × 100
- Decided to calculate crude readmission rates only when both numeric readmission counts and valid discharge counts are available.
- Decided not to treat N/A as zero readmissions.
- Decided not to calculate readmission rates for Too Few to Report records because their discharge counts are unavailable.
- Decided not to calculate upper-bound readmission rates for suppressed records because the original dataset provides N/A for their discharge denominators.
- Removed upper-bound rate calculations from the planned final analysis table.
- Retained Excess Readmission Ratio, Predicted Readmission Rate, and Expected Readmission Rate as separate HRRP measures.
- Distinguished calculated crude readmission rates from official CMS risk-standardized measures.
- Decided that <10 must be displayed as a suppressed count, not interpreted as evidence of better hospital performance.
- Recognized that comparisons across conditions must account for differences in patient populations and measure definitions.
- Decided to use hospital ratings as supporting hospital information rather than as a replacement for readmission performance metrics.
5. Final analysis table structure
- Created public.hospital_readmission_analysis to combine hospital information and HRRP data.
- Designed the table to contain one row per hospital.
- Converted the six HRRP conditions from separate rows into separate column groups.
- Joined hospital information and HRRP data using Facility ID.
- Used a LEFT JOIN so hospitals in the hospital information table are retained even when HRRP records are unavailable.
- Created separate column groups for AMI, CABG, COPD, Heart Failure, Hip/Knee Replacement, and Pneumonia.
- Retained discharge counts for each condition.
- Retained numeric readmission counts for each condition.
- Retained separate readmission display-status columns for each condition.
- Retained calculated crude readmission rates for each condition.
- Retained Excess Readmission Ratio for each condition.
- Retained Predicted Readmission Rate for each condition.
- Retained Expected Readmission Rate for each condition.
- Excluded upper-bound readmission-rate columns from the final design.
